  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>US CENTCOM struck Larak Island — Iranian sovereign territory in the eastern Strait of Hormuz — on Aug 30, 2026, in what CENTCOM Capt. Tim Hawkins confirmed was a deliberate strike on Iranian military assets. Washington Post, CNN, Al Jazeera, and CNBC all carried independent confirmation within 12 hours. Iran's IRGC responded on Aug 31 with approximately 32 ballistic missiles targeting US military installations at King Hussein Air Base and Al-Azraq base in Jordan, confirmed by Gulf News, Jerusalem Post, and ANI News. The Aug 2 ceasefire that had held for 29 days ended on Day 184 (Aug 30). → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>The Larak strike landed while Iran's MFA was still managing the Mojtaba Khamenei "real enemy" letter (Aug 30) — a written message to Gulf rulers via Telegram calling on them to identify and confront their "real enemy," explicitly naming the US and Israel. The letter was confirmed by Haaretz, NBC News, and Jerusalem Post. Per Axios (Aug 5), Araghchi had agreed to the interim Hormuz deal framework but required Mojtaba's explicit approval before proceeding to formalization; as of Aug 31, no source attributes an approval of the Hormuz framework to Mojtaba. The combined effect of the Larak strike and the gatekeeper's posture collapses the probability of a formal US-Iran Hormuz agreement before Sep 14 from 25% to 8%, and of a formal MFA conditions statement before Sep 2 from 45% to 27%. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Larak Island's targeting is significant for the transit recovery trajectory: the strike demonstrated that IRGC has the capacity to operate against the Hormuz corridor even after CENTCOM's Aug 27 mine-free declaration. Lloyd's List and P&I underwriters, who were already withholding war-risk premium reinstatement despite the mine-free announcement, now face direct evidence that cleared lanes can be re-contested without warning. USNI News (Aug 28) recorded 16.3 vessels/day in the Aug 17-24 window — above the 15/day partial threshold (now CONFIRMED) — but 47% below the 24/day YES target. The Larak re-contestation of cleared space materially lowers the probability of reaching the 24/day threshold by Oct 15. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Two forecasts are retired this cycle. F5 (ceasefire holds through Sep 30) resolved NO: both a US strike on Iranian sovereign territory (Larak) and an Iranian strike on US military facilities (Jordan) occurred within 24 hours, each independently meeting the resolution trigger. F7 (Netanyahu coalition collapse by Oct 27) resolved YES retroactively: the Knesset's 62-0 self-dissolution vote on Jul 17, 2026 constituted a dissolution event with date earlier than Oct 27, satisfying the resolution criteria under its most natural textual reading. F7 was incorrectly retained at p=0.10 following Sunday's cold-start audit; the DA hygiene review identified this as a tracking error and the Judge accepted the correction. A new forecast, F8, opens at p=0.38 tracking whether a strike pause can re-establish before Sep 14. → moved <span class="cite">F7</span>, <span class="cite">F8</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP (computed summary) ========== -->
