      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> F5 RESOLVED-NO drives this to dominant: with the ceasefire collapsed, F2 at 8%, F3 at 22%, and F4 at 22%, a sustained or widening exchange is the base case.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> F8 at 38% anchors this — both sides' costs from the Larak/Jordan exchange create structural incentive for a pause that is neither a ceasefire nor a full escalation, with mediator channels nominally active.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> F1 at 27% and F2 at 8% leave a residual de-escalation path only if Iran issues a formal conditions statement in the next 48h and Mojtaba's approval posture shifts; the Larak strike makes this the least likely outcome at 13%.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Mojtaba Khamenei published a letter to Gulf rulers via Telegram on Aug 30 under the rubric of Islamic Unity Week. The letter called on Gulf states to "identify your real enemy" — explicitly naming the US and Israel — and confront it, framing internal Muslim division as serving adversary interests. Mojtaba has had no confirmed public appearance since being injured in strikes approximately six months ago; written Telegram statements are his primary communications channel. The letter carried no Hormuz-deal language, no framework-approval posture, and no reference to the Oman-mediated corridor negotiations. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Iranian state media (IRNA, Tasnim, Fars) framed the Larak Island strike as an act of aggression on Iranian sovereign territory requiring an official response. The MFA issued statements through Baghaei as its formal spokesperson, but none of the Aug 30-31 statements attributed to MFA channels enumerated specific named conditions for Hormuz reopening; the public MFA posture in response to Larak is of the general-declaration category, not the formal enumerated-conditions press-conference type the F1 resolution criterion requires. → supports <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>The IRGC's Aug 31 ballistic missile operation against US bases in Jordan — King Hussein Air Base and Al-Azraq — is characterized in IRGC statements as a proportionate military response to the Larak strike on sovereign territory. The IRGC operational posture of executing a ballistic missile strike at this scale is relevant for the strike-pause question: the retaliatory strike was pre-planned in structure and executed within 24h, which is consistent with standing contingency planning rather than improvised escalation. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

